Vendored MD5 (public domain)
============================

Source:   Alexander Peslyak (Solar Designer), "OpenSSL-compatible MD5", 2001.
          http://openwall.info/wiki/people/solar/software/public-domain-source-code/md5
Licence:  public domain (fallback: permissive, "redistribution and use ... are permitted"); see the header of md5.c.
Used by:  the archive download (M3), to detect a truncated or corrupted download.

Upstream: md5/md5.c and md5/md5.h from https://github.com/openwall/popa3d (the same file the homepage links).
md5.c is byte-identical to upstream. md5.h only gained extern "C" guards for C++ (marked "OpenShop").
On x86 upstream reads words directly; on the big-endian Wii it uses the portable byte-wise SET(). The MD5 itself is
checked by tests/install_test.cpp (RFC 1321 vectors, and chunked versus one-shot hashing).
The PC tests run the x86 word-read branch; the byte-wise branch the Wii uses was checked once by hand (the same
tests with that #if forced off, under ASan/UBSan) and gets real coverage from the first Wii download (M3 Increment 8).

MD5 here detects corruption only. Archives come over plain http, so it says nothing about tampering.
